# Literature

# Developer Notes

Both monolithic and staggered NLBGS cases use two time steps and compare final
adjoints against the same stored reference. Objective and constraint gradients
are checked against forward, backward, and central finite differences. The
previous-state contribution is assembled for all state DOFs once after each
complete adjoint solve, then held fixed during the next backward step. This
example exercises thermal time continuity coupled to mechanical displacement.


An isolated PETSc staggered regression uses the same mesh, material, loading,
nonlinear tolerances and FD comparisons, with managed GMRES settings. It verifies
full-state temporal history restriction into thermal/displacement subsystem
maps. Both backends use the same stored objective, constraint and gradient
references, obtained from the two-step Epetra staggered case on one rank.
Set gPrintReferenceValues in the driver to inspect these values. Epetra also
checks the stored full-adjoint vector; PETSc has a different vector-file
representation. This workflow runs on one rank; distributed transfer regressions run
on one, two and four ranks in DLA-PETSc-Adjoint-test.

*/

